// Does this `.tutu` file mean what the `.html` file beside it meant?
//
// The acceptance check for converting a view file, and the reason a conversion
// can be trusted at all. A `.tutu` file is not "the same file in a new
// notation" because it LOOKS equivalent; it is the same file because the things
// the pipeline reads out of it are the same things — the schema, the handlers,
// the trees, the fixtures and the scenes.
//
// So this compares those, not the text. Each section is read by the reader that
// consumes it downstream, on both sides, and the results are compared:
//
//   spec      `@statedef.StateDef`, as the splitter parsed it
//   logic     `@tscript.Decl`s, as the script parser parsed them
//   view      `@anode.ANode`, modulo whitespace (`@toanode.strip_ws`) and
//             modulo how an operator is spelled (`@toanode.flatten_ops`) — a
//             slot parser reads `(.a is 'x')` as an application and the block
//             grammar reads `a == b` as a chain, and `eval_chain` reduces one
//             to the other before evaluating either
//   fixtures  `@statedef.InitState`
//   tests     the parsed scenes
//
// **What it cannot see, and cannot be widened to see.** Every comparison here
// is between two things a READER produced, so anything that only goes wrong at
// RENDER time is outside it by construction rather than by oversight. The sharp
// case is a protocol view binding: `Editable@1::editor` going missing makes the
// renderer fall back to `main`, which draws something plausible — the trees this
// compares are equal and the editor role is gone. A conversion that touches
// protocol bindings has to assert them BY NAME; a clean run here is not evidence
// about them. The same boundary covers anything else whose failure is a
// fallback rather than a different tree.
//
// The `view` half is deliberately asymmetric: the `.html` side goes through the
// HTML parser, and the `.tutu` side goes through `tutufile/toanode` — the
// DIRECT reader, not the printer. That is what makes this check worth running
// over the corpus twice over: it verifies a conversion, and it exercises the
// reader that will replace the printer, on every view the repo has.
import {
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/tutufile",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/tutufile/lower",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/tutufile/toanode",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/tutufile/names",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/viewfile",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/anode",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/core" @tutuca,
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/tscript",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/statedef",
  "marianoguerra/error-report" @report,
  "moonbitlang/core/debug",
}
